  /* what students of coups have found, set beside what this archive's own data shows */
  findings: [
    ["Poverty", "The poorer the country, the likelier a coup — the most robust finding in the literature. In this record the poorest quarter of country-years saw a coup attempt about thirteen times as often as the richest."],
    ["The coup trap", "A coup makes the next one likelier: it teaches officers that it can be done and leaves a government with no title but force."],
    ["The middle is dangerous", "Closed dictatorships and consolidated democracies are both hard to overthrow. The exposed regimes are the hybrids in between — and since 1950 no coup attempt at all is recorded in a country V-Dem classed as a liberal democracy the year before."],
    ["After the Cold War", "Coups fell by more than half after 1990, and those that happened were more often followed by elections: neither superpower would any longer underwrite a junta."],
    ["The Sahel exception", "The return of the coup after 2020 is concentrated in one belt of poor states fighting insurgencies they cannot defeat."]
  ],
